   "text": "You are comparing two drafts written from the same brief. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; judge only the text below.\n\nBrief: Write the landing page copy for `portlog`, following the tool page section order.\n\n<<<DRAFT A\n# See which process held a port\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time.\n\nUse it to answer “what was on port 3000 an hour ago?” after the process has exited.\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nmacOS and Linux · Written in Go · MIT license\n\n## Start recording\n\nStart the background recorder:\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nShow every process that held port 3000, newest first:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\n## Commands\n\nLimit the history to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\nStop the process that holds port 3000 now. portlog asks for confirmation first.\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nTo skip confirmation, add `-y`:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000 -y\n```\n\nStop the recorder:\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n## Good to know\n\n### History\n\nportlog keeps history for 7 days in `~/.portlog/`.\n\n### Command-line arguments\n\nportlog never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.\nDRAFT A>>>\n\n<<<DRAFT B\n# portlog\n\nKnow what held a local TCP port—even after the process has exited.\n\n“What was on port 3000 an hour ago?” portlog records which process held each port over time, so you can look back.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nRuns on macOS and Linux.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Start recording\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nRuns the recorder in the background.\n\n### Look up a port\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nSee every process that held port 3000, newest first—even processes that have exited.\n\nLimit the results to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n### Free a port\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nStop the process holding port 3000 now, after confirmation. Add `-y` to skip confirmation.\n\n### Stop recording\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n## How it works\n\nStart the background recorder, then query the history when you need it. portlog keeps seven days of history in `~/.portlog/`.\n\nCommand-line arguments can hold secrets. portlog never records them.\n\n## Details\n\nWritten in Go. MIT license.\nDRAFT B>>>\n\nAnswer each question with \"A\", \"B\", or \"SAME\":\n- \"reader\": Could a first-time reader do what they came to do?\n- \"careful\": Does it read like a careful person wrote it?\n- \"overall\": Which draft is better overall?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"reader\": \"A\", \"careful\": \"A\", \"overall\": \"A\", \"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"}",
